  <h2>The Trap of Over-Optimization</h2>
  <p>
    There is a real risk in bringing data into the bedroom: <em>orthosomnia</em>, the anxious preoccupation with sleep metrics that can paradoxically worsen sleep. The person who checks their sleep score immediately upon waking, who cannot relax until they know their HRV was "good," who adjusts their entire evening around optimizing one metric — this person is not sleeping better. They are introducing exactly the kind of performance anxiety that elevates cortisol and fragments sleep.
  </p>
  <p>
    SleepTech's design philosophy on this point is explicit: the data should be reviewed weekly, not nightly. The morning glance at the previous night's summary should take thirty seconds, not thirty minutes. Trend views over seven or thirty days are more meaningful than any single night's score. The goal of the data is to identify which of your habits are genuinely working and to detect changes that warrant attention — not to create a new source of bedtime anxiety.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you find that checking your sleep data before bed elevates your anxiety, the protocol is simple: check it only after breakfast, treat it as information rather than judgment, and remember that the most important predictor of good sleep tonight is not last night's score — it is what you do in the next hour.
  </p>

  <h2>Building Consistency: The Behavioral Architecture</h2>
  <p>
    The research on habit formation consistently shows that consistency matters more than intensity. A moderate wind-down routine practiced every night for a month produces more durable sleep improvement than an elaborate ritual followed intermittently. Habit formation studies suggest that the behavioral trigger — the environmental cue that initiates the routine — is as important as the routine itself. For sleep, this typically means a consistent first action (dimming the lights, making tea, putting on specific music) that serves as the behavioral anchor for everything that follows.
  </p>
  <p>
    The YumePad data can be used to reinforce consistency behaviorally: reviewing your weekly sleep trend on Sunday morning and observing that your best sleep nights clustered around routine-adherent days creates a positive feedback loop that is more motivating than any amount of willpower or abstract health knowledge. The data makes the benefit concrete and personal, which is precisely what generic advice cannot do.
  </p>

